<!-- std.fmt.md -->
# `std.fmt`

`std.fmt` (11): formatting. The traits `Display` (what `{x}` writes)
and `Debug` (what `{x:?}` writes), the `Formatter` they write into and
the `Spec` of a placeholder (width, fill, alignment, precision) are the
prelude's, which interpolation uses, and are reached from here too
(`from std.fmt import Display`).

## Re-exports

- `Display`: `std.core.Display`
- `Debug`: `std.core.Debug`
- `Formatter`: `std.core.Formatter`
- `Spec`: `std.core.Spec`
<!-- std.string.md -->
# `std.string`

`std.string` (11): text. A `String` is its UTF-8 bytes, `str` is the
type of a text literal, and `char` is a Unicode scalar value; their
methods (`len`, `chars`, `split`, `trim`, `find`, `replace`,
`to_lowercase` and `to_uppercase`, which map case over all of Unicode,
...) are the prelude's, and `String` is reached from here too (`from
std.string import String`; `str` and `char` are built-in names).
`Builder` makes a text from many pieces. Grapheme iteration and the
normalisation forms need Unicode's tables, which are not here yet.

## Re-exports

- `String`: `std.core.String`

## Contents

- [`Builder`](#Builder): A text made from many pieces: each `push` keeps its piece, and `build` copies them all into one `String` once, so making a long text from short pieces copies each byte once.

<a id="Builder"></a>
## `Builder`

```sisu
pub struct Builder
    pieces: list[String]
    bytes: int
```

A text made from many pieces: each `push` keeps its piece, and `build`
copies them all into one `String` once, so making a long text from
short pieces copies each byte once.

```sisu
var b = Builder.new()
b.push("one")
b.push_char(',')
b.push(" two")
b.push_line("")
b.push_line("three")
assert b.build() == "one, two\nthree\n"
assert b.byte_len() == 15
```

<a id="Builder.new"></a>
### `Builder.new`

```sisu
pub pure def new() -> Builder
```

An empty builder.

<a id="Builder.push"></a>
### `Builder.push`

```sisu
pub pure def push(mut self, text: str)
```

Adds `text`.

<a id="Builder.push_char"></a>
### `Builder.push_char`

```sisu
pub pure def push_char(mut self, c: char)
```

Adds the character `c`.

<a id="Builder.push_line"></a>
### `Builder.push_line`

```sisu
pub pure def push_line(mut self, text: str)
```

Adds `text` and then a line break.

<a id="Builder.push_joined"></a>
### `Builder.push_joined`

```sisu
pub pure def push_joined(mut self, parts: list[str], sep: str)
```

Adds the pieces of `parts` with `sep` between each two.

```sisu
var b = Builder.new()
b.push("[")
b.push_joined(["a", "b", "c"], "; ")
b.push("]")
assert b.build() == "[a; b; c]"
```

<a id="Builder.byte_len"></a>
### `Builder.byte_len`

```sisu
pub pure def byte_len(self) -> int
```

The number of bytes of the text so far.

<a id="Builder.is_empty"></a>
### `Builder.is_empty`

```sisu
pub pure def is_empty(self) -> bool
```

<a id="Builder.build"></a>
### `Builder.build`

```sisu
pub pure def build(self) -> String
```

The text so far, as one `String`; the builder keeps its pieces.
<!-- std.time.md -->
# `std.time`

`std.time` (11): `DateTime`, `Duration`, `Timer`, `TimeError` and the
test clock `TestClock` are the prelude's, which this module also names;
`Ticker` and `Timeline` (N-35, D-075) are written here, over a `Clock`,
so the same code keeps real time in production and is deterministic
under the test clock.

## Re-exports

- `DateTime`: `std.core.DateTime`
- `Duration`: `std.core.Duration`
- `Timer`: `std.core.Timer`
- `TimeError`: `std.core.TimeError`
- `TestClock`: `std.core.TestClock`
- `Clock`: `std.core.Clock`

## Contents

- [`Ticker`](#Ticker): A periodic ticker that does not drift (N-35): its `n`-th tick is at `start + n * period` whatever each iteration took, so a slow iteration does not push the ticks after it back. A tick that passes while the loop is busy is skipped, not fired late, and counted in `missed`.
- [`Timeline`](#Timeline): Virtual time (Sonic Pi's model, N-35): `sleep(d)` moves the timeline's own time on by exactly `d` and then waits for the clock to reach it, so the time spent between sleeps does not accumulate as drift.

<a id="Ticker"></a>
## `Ticker`

```sisu
pub struct Ticker
    clock: Clock
    period: Duration
    next: DateTime
    ## The ticks skipped because they passed while the loop was busy.
    pub missed: int
```

A periodic ticker that does not drift (N-35): its `n`-th tick is at
`start + n * period` whatever each iteration took, so a slow iteration
does not push the ticks after it back. A tick that passes while the
loop is busy is skipped, not fired late, and counted in `missed`.

Each `wait` is a boundary where a running program adopts the newest
epoch of hot replacement (10, "Debugging and profiling": `Ticker.wait`
calls `live.boundary()`).

```sisu
let clock = Clock.frozen_at(DateTime.parse("2026-01-01T00:00:00Z").expect("a time"))
let start = clock.now()
var t = Ticker.every(clock, Duration.seconds(1))
t.wait().expect("not cancelled")
t.wait().expect("not cancelled")
assert clock.now() - start == Duration.seconds(2)
clock.advance(Duration.millis(3500))
t.wait().expect("not cancelled")
assert clock.now() - start == Duration.seconds(6)
assert t.missed == 3
```

<a id="Ticker.every"></a>
### `Ticker.every`

```sisu
pub def every(clock: Clock, period: Duration) -> Ticker
    requires period > Duration.seconds(0)
```

Ticks every `period`, the first a period from now.

Capabilities: `clock` (`Clock`).

<a id="Ticker.wait"></a>
### `Ticker.wait`

```sisu
pub def wait(mut self) -> ! TaskError
```

Waits for the next tick still ahead, skipping those that passed.

Capabilities: `self` (`Clock`).

<a id="Timeline"></a>
## `Timeline`

```sisu
pub struct Timeline
    clock: Clock
    ## The timeline's time: where it is in its score.
    pub now: DateTime
```

Virtual time (Sonic Pi's model, N-35): `sleep(d)` moves the timeline's
own time on by exactly `d` and then waits for the clock to reach it, so
the time spent between sleeps does not accumulate as drift.

```sisu
let clock = Clock.frozen_at(DateTime.parse("2026-01-01T00:00:00Z").expect("a time"))
var tl = Timeline.start(clock)
let t0 = tl.now
tl.sleep(Duration.millis(250)).expect("not cancelled")
clock.advance(Duration.millis(100))
tl.sleep(Duration.millis(250)).expect("not cancelled")
assert tl.now - t0 == Duration.millis(500)
assert clock.now() - t0 == Duration.millis(500)
```

<a id="Timeline.start"></a>
### `Timeline.start`

```sisu
pub def start(clock: Clock) -> Timeline
```

A timeline whose time starts at the clock's.

Capabilities: `clock` (`Clock`).

<a id="Timeline.sleep"></a>
### `Timeline.sleep`

```sisu
pub def sleep(mut self, d: Duration) -> ! TaskError
```

Moves the timeline on by `d`, and waits until the clock is there.

Capabilities: `self` (`Clock`).
